<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="activity-limit-definition">
  <title>The Definition of a Limit</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The limit <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a} f(x)</m> is a value <m>L</m> if the value of <m>f(x)</m> converges to <m>L</m> when <m>x \to a</m> from the left and right. The value of <m>f(a)</m> itself is not relevant. We need a way to formalize what this means. Rules of limits will eventually all be based on the definition that we choose.
    </p>
    <p>
      In order to be precise, we need to be able to say what it means for <m>f(x)</m> to <em>converge</em> to a value <m>L</m> as <m>x \to a</m>. When we looked at limits using a table, we saw that the value of <m>f(x)</m> was becoming <em>closer</em> to a final value when <m>x</m> was getting <em>closer</em> to <m>a</m>. Limits are based on the idea of <em>distance</em> between values.
    </p>
    <p>
      For two numbers <m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m>, the <term>distance</term> is measured as the absolute value of their difference, <m>|x_1 - x_2|</m>. In a limit statement, we are interested in two different distances at the same time: <m>|x-a|</m> measures the distance <m>x</m> is away from <m>a</m> and <m>|f(x) - L|</m> measures the distance <m>f(x)</m> is away from <m>L</m>.
    </p>
    <assemblage>
      <title>Definition of a Limit</title>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a} f(x) = L</m> means that <q>for every <m>\epsilon \gt 0</m>, there exists some <m>\delta \gt 0</m> so that <m>|f(x)-L| \lt \epsilon</m> whenever <m>0 \lt |x-a| \lt \delta</m>.</q>
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <p>
      Limits are really about approximation. The value of the limit <m>L</m> represents some value that we want to know but that in principle is something we can only approximate. The function <m>f(x)</m> is the rule of approximation based on choosing values of <m>x</m> that get progressively closer to <m>a</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The inequality <m>|f(x)-L| \lt \epsilon</m> means <m>L-\epsilon \lt f(x) \lt L+\epsilon</m>. The value <m>\epsilon</m> represents an acceptable tolerance of error in our approximation, or how close we require <m>f(x)</m> to be to <m>L</m>. The inequality <m>|x-a| \lt \delta</m> means <m>a-\delta \lt x \lt a+\delta</m>. The additional requirement <m>0 \lt |x-a|</m> means <m>x \ne a</m>. The value <m>\delta</m> represents how close <m>x</m> needs to be to <m>a</m> in order to be <em>guaranteed</em> that <m>f(x)</m> is within <m>\epsilon</m> of <m>L</m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-limit-def-abs-value" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 1: Absolute Value Inequalities</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        An absolute value inequality such as <m>|x-a| \lt r</m> says that the distance from <m>x</m> to <m>a</m> is less than <m>r</m>. Equivalently, <m>a-r \lt x \lt a+r</m>, which is an interval centered at <m>a</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For each statement below, answer the following.
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p>Write an absolute value inequality that represents the statement.</p></li>
          <li><p>Write the set of values that satisfy the inequality as an interval.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-limit-def-distance-two" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <q>The distance that <m>x</m> is away from 2 is less than 0.1.</q>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-limit-def-mass" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <q>The mass <m>m</m> (g) of an object is within 0.05 grams of 10 grams.</q>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-limit-def-height" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <q>The height <m>h</m> (m) of a plant is within 0.01 m of 2.4 m.</q>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-limit-def-function-distance" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 2: Distance Between Function Values</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The same idea applies to outputs. The distance between the output <m>f(x)</m> and the value <m>L</m> is <m>|f(x)-L|</m>. If we write <m>y=f(x)</m>, then the inequality <m>|y-L| \lt \epsilon</m> describes a horizontal strip around the line <m>y=L</m>. The graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> is inside the strip exactly for the <m>x</m>-values where <m>|f(x)-L| \lt \epsilon</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        In this section, let <m>f(x) = 2x+3</m> and <m>L=7</m>. Notice that <m>f(2)=7</m>, so the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> crosses the line <m>y=L</m> at <m>x=2</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-limit-def-linear-ineq" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write an absolute value inequality that represents <q>the distance between <m>f(x)</m> and <m>L</m> is less than 0.2</q>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-limit-def-linear-y-interval" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write the absolute value inequality <q><m>y</m> is within 0.2 units of 7</q> and interpret it as an interval of <m>y</m>-values.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-limit-def-linear-graph" workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch the graph of <m>y=f(x)=2x+3</m> and the horizontal line <m>y=7</m>. Then draw a horizontal strip surrounding that line that represents <m>|y-7| \lt 0.2</m>. Label the <m>y</m>-values of the lower and upper edges of the strip. (The strip is narrow, so your sketch does not need to be to scale.)
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-limit-def-linear-x-interval" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Identify the <m>x</m>-values for which <m>|f(x)-7| \lt 0.2</m>. These are the <m>x</m>-values for which the graph of <m>y=f(x)</m> is inside the strip. Write them as an interval.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-limit-def-linear-centered" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The graph crosses <m>y=7</m> at <m>x=2</m>. Is the interval of <m>x</m>-values you found centered at <m>x=2</m>? How far does it extend to the left of <m>x=2</m> and to the right of <m>x=2</m>? Write your answer as an absolute value inequality of the form <m>|x-2| \lt \text{(number)}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-limit-def-quadratic" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 3: A Function That Is Not Linear</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Now repeat the process with <m>f(x) = x^2</m> and <m>L=4</m>, using the distance <m>\epsilon = 0.1</m>. Notice that <m>f(2)=4</m>, so we again center our attention on <m>x=2</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-limit-def-quadratic-ineq" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write an absolute value inequality that represents <q>the distance between <m>f(x)</m> and 4 is less than 0.1</q>, and rewrite it as a compound inequality for <m>f(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-limit-def-quadratic-intersections" workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use a graphing calculator to graph <m>y=x^2</m>, <m>y=3.9</m>, and <m>y=4.1</m>. Find the <m>x</m>-coordinates of the two intersection points near <m>x=2</m>. Record each with at least 4 decimal places, and then write the interval of <m>x</m>-values near 2 for which the graph is inside the strip <m>|y-4| \lt 0.1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-limit-def-quadratic-asymmetric" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p>How far is the left endpoint of your interval from <m>x=2</m>? How far is the right endpoint from <m>x=2</m>?</p></li>
          <li><p>Is your interval symmetric around <m>x=2</m>?</p></li>
          <li><p>What is the widest distance <m>\delta</m> from <m>x=2</m> so that every <m>x</m> with <m>|x-2| \lt \delta</m> stays inside your interval?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-limit-def-epsilon-delta" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 4: Describing the Limit Definition</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        In the previous sections you carried out the key step of the limit definition for one choice of <m>\epsilon</m>. In general, the definition describes the following process, which is illustrated in <xref ref="fig-limit-def-epsilon-delta"/>.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-limit-def-epsilon-delta">
        <caption>A strip <m>|y-L| \lt \epsilon</m> and a symmetric neighborhood <m>|x-a| \lt \delta</m> for <m>f(x)=x^2</m>, <m>a=2</m>, and <m>L=4</m>. The value of <m>\epsilon</m> is chosen large so the picture is readable. The half-filled point at <m>x=a</m> indicates that the limit ignores whether <m>f(a)=L</m> or not.</caption>
        <image xml:id="image-limit-def-epsilon-delta" width="60%">
          <shortdescription>
            The graph of an upward-curving parabola passing through the point (2,4). A horizontal
            shaded strip surrounds the line y=4, with edges labeled L minus epsilon and L plus epsilon
            on the y-axis. Two vertical dashed lines mark the interval from a minus delta to a plus delta
            on the x-axis, centered at a=2. Within that interval the graph stays inside the strip. The point
            on the graph at x=a is drawn half-filled, to show that it may or may not be part of the graph.
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=6cm, y=1.1cm, >=stealth]
              % strip
              \fill[blue!12] (1.5,3) rectangle (2.5,5);
              \draw[dashed] (1.5,3) -- (2.5,3);
              \draw[dashed] (1.5,5) -- (2.5,5);
              \draw[dashed] (1.5,4) -- (2,4);
              % axes
              \draw[->] (1.5,2.5) -- (2.5,2.5) node[right] {$x$};
              \draw[->] (1.5,2.5) -- (1.5,5.6) node[above] {$y$};
              % graph
              \draw[thick, domain=1.6:2.34, samples=60] plot (\x, {\x*\x});
              % delta neighborhood: a-delta = 4-sqrt(5), a+delta = sqrt(5)
              \draw[dashed] (1.7639,2.5) -- (1.7639,3.1115);
              \draw[dashed] (2.2361,2.5) -- (2.2361,5);
              \draw[dashed] (2,2.5) -- (2,4);
              \draw[line width=2pt, red!70!black] (1.7639,2.5) -- (2.2361,2.5);
              \draw[line width=2pt, red!70!black] (1.5,3) -- (1.5,5);
              % half-filled point at x=a: f(a) may or may not equal L
              \begin{scope}[shift={(2,4)}, x=1pt, y=1pt]
                \fill (0,0) ++(0,3) arc (90:270:3) -- cycle;
                \draw (0,0) circle (3);
              \end{scope}
              % labels
              \node[below] at (1.7639,2.5) {$a-\delta$};
              \node[below] at (2,2.5) {$a$};
              \node[below] at (2.2361,2.5) {$a+\delta$};
              \node[left] at (1.5,3) {$L-\epsilon$};
              \node[left] at (1.5,4) {$L$};
              \node[left] at (1.5,5) {$L+\epsilon$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The point on the graph at <m>x=a</m> is drawn half-filled on purpose. The limit ignores the value of <m>f(a)</m>, so the point may or may not be included in the graph at <m>x=a</m>. Only the behavior of the graph near <m>x=a</m> matters.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Given <em>any</em> value of <m>\epsilon \gt 0</m>, we can create the strip <m>|y-L| \lt \epsilon</m> and look at <m>y=f(x)</m> to find the set of <m>x</m>-values for which <m>|f(x)-L| \lt \epsilon</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>We then verify that <m>x=a</m> is <em>inside</em> an interval within this set, possibly excluding the actual value of <m>a</m> itself. The value <m>\delta \gt 0</m> represents a symmetric neighborhood <m>a-\delta \lt x \lt a+\delta</m> with <m>x \ne a</m> completely contained in the solution set of <m>|f(x)-L| \lt \epsilon</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>If this works for <em>every</em> positive choice for <m>\epsilon</m>, then the limit statement is true. If there is <em>any</em> value of <m>\epsilon</m> such that this fails to work, then the limit statement is false.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-limit-def-linear-delta" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For <m>f(x)=2x+3</m>, <m>a=2</m>, and <m>L=7</m>, you found in Section 2 the set of <m>x</m>-values that works for <m>\epsilon=0.2</m>. What is the largest value of <m>\delta</m> that works? Would a smaller positive value of <m>\delta</m> also work? Explain.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-limit-def-quadratic-delta" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For <m>f(x)=x^2</m>, <m>a=2</m>, and <m>L=4</m>, use your work in Section 3 to give a value of <m>\delta</m> that works for <m>\epsilon=0.1</m>. Then explain why <m>\delta=0.03</m> does <em>not</em> work by finding a specific <m>x</m> with <m>|x-2| \lt 0.03</m> for which <m>|f(x)-4| \lt 0.1</m> is false.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-limit-def-false-claim" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose someone claims that <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 2} (2x+3) = 5</m>. Test the claim with <m>\epsilon = 0.2</m>.
        </p>
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p>Find the set of <m>x</m>-values for which <m>|(2x+3)-5| \lt 0.2</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Is <m>x=2</m> inside an interval within this set? Is there any value of <m>\delta \gt 0</m> that works?</p></li>
          <li><p>What does this tell you about the claim?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-limit-def-reciprocal-claim" workspace="2.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose someone claims that <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 3} \frac{2}{x} = 0.6</m>. Test the claim with two different tolerances, using a graph or by solving the inequalities.
        </p>
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p>For <m>\epsilon = 0.1</m>, find the set of <m>x</m>-values for which <m>\left| \frac{2}{x} - 0.6 \right| \lt 0.1</m>. Is <m>x=3</m> inside an interval within this set? If so, give a value of <m>\delta</m> that works.</p></li>
          <li><p>For <m>\epsilon = 0.05</m>, find the set of <m>x</m>-values for which <m>\left| \frac{2}{x} - 0.6 \right| \lt 0.05</m>. Is <m>x=3</m> inside an interval within this set? Is there any value of <m>\delta \gt 0</m> that works?</p></li>
          <li><p>Is the claim true or false? Explain why a choice of <m>\delta</m> that works for one value of <m>\epsilon</m> is not enough.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
